      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ETFs typically offer more immediate liquidity due to intraday trading mechanisms, whereas mutual funds settle prices only after market close and may involve redemption delays. Management-wise, ETFs often utilize passively managed index strategies or programmatic active management, while mutual funds frequently employ active management for custom portfolio tailoring to specific client goals. Below is a comparison highlighting these key distinctions:\n\n| Feature | ETFs (Exchange-Traded Funds) | Mutual Funds |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Liquidity** | Trades throughout the day at market prices; settled T+1. | Trades once daily at End-of-Day (EOD) NAV; settled T+2. |\n| **Management Style** | Often passively managed; some actively managed via algorithms. | Typically active management to meet specific investment objectives. |\n| **Pricing** | Real-time market price (bid/ask spread). | Calculated based on dividend/interest held at EOD. |",
